Planets gain heat from stellar radiation, from radioactive decay of 
isotopes in the crust and mantel, and from the continuing (primordial) heat of 
contraction.  For example, Mars has much less heavy metal than Earth, and insufficient mass
to generate much heat from its continued contraction; its interior
is now solid.  In contrast, the interiors of Jupiter and Saturn are not solid because their
continued gravitational contraction produce so much heat.

